 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Text: Speaking the language of my grandmother\nWhen Ngum visited her grandmother in Kom, she felt like a stranger. Grandmother spoke only the Kom language, and Ngum, who had grown up in Yaoundé, spoke English and French. They smiled at each other but could not share stories. Back in town, Ngum asked her father to teach her. Every evening they practised greetings, proverbs and songs. At school, she joined the national languages class. The next holiday, she greeted her grandmother in Kom. The old woman cried with joy and told her the story of their family until midnight. “A language is a key,” Ngum wrote in her diary. “It opens the door to my people's wisdom.”\n\nCameroon has more than 250 national languages (Ewondo, Duala, Fulfulde, Kom…) and two official languages, English and French. A mother tongue is the first language learnt at home.",
  "checks": []
 },
